WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:03.670
Opinions about hypersonic weapons are not
peaceful.

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:08.630
Some people consider them "game changers"
because, given their extreme speed, it would

00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:13.960
be impossible to defend against their attack;
others say they are but the latest in a long

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:19.070
line of overhyped weapons systems and are
relatively easy to defend against because

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:25.369
they fly more slowly as they near their target
than ballistic missiles with càmparable ranges.

00:00:25.369 --> 00:00:30.660
Others claim that they will strengthen conventional
deterrence; still others argue they will create

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:35.470
instability between nuclear-armed nations
by fueling a dangerous arms race.

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:40.650
This debate has gained greater attention in
recent years as the United States, Russia

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:43.730
and China have made their own progress in
the field.

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:49.200
But before explaining what the three powers
have in store, we need to explain what a hypersonic

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:52.880
missile is and differentiate it from a ballistic
missile.

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:58.160
We are helped by various entities including
the Council for International Affairs of Russia

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:03.290
(but also the American Missile Defense Advocacy
Alliance) that in an article states that "there

00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:09.039
are two requirements to label a weapon as
hypersonic: a speed greater than Mach 5, so

00:01:09.039 --> 00:01:14.380
more than five times that of sound and the
ability to change course and perform maneuvers

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:18.840
(both vertical and horizontal) while remaining
within the atmosphere.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:24.039
This differentiates it from ballistic missiles
that instead travel outside the atmosphere

00:01:24.039 --> 00:01:29.880
and although they can reach hypersonic speeds
(and very often do), they have fixed arc trajectories

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:31.310
and poor maneuverability.

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:33.729
This image is worth a thousand words.

00:01:33.729 --> 00:01:38.270
An example of these is the Minuteman III,
the U.S. intercontinental ballistic missile

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:42.240
(ICBM), armed with nuclear warheads, which
can reach Mach 23 on descent and could strike

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:48.279
Russia from silos located in the western United
States within about 30 minutes from launch.

00:01:48.279 --> 00:01:53.569
Traditional cruise missiles, on the other
hand, fly at subsonic speeds (less than Mach

00:01:53.569 --> 00:01:57.520
1) and supersonic speeds (between Mach 1 and
Mach 5).

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:03.189
The hypersonic missiles then divide into two
species: hypersonic cruise missiles (HCM)

00:02:03.189 --> 00:02:05.310
and hypersonic glide vehicles (HGV).

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:10.509
The former are powered by high-speed engines,
called scramjets, and thanks to them they

00:02:10.509 --> 00:02:15.450
keep a constant hypersonic speed and altitude
during the entire flight.

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:20.330
In contrast, the latter, since they have no
propulsion system, are usually launched on

00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:24.370
top of a booster rocket (boost-glide system)
and then glide through the atmosphere toward

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:26.810
their target at hypersonic speeds.

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:30.110
Both types can carry conventional or nuclear
payloads.

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:34.540
The advantage of these missiles over their
ballistic rivals is quite obvious.

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:40.220
As reported by the expert Cameron Tracy, “since
hypersonic weapons fly through the atmosphere,

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:45.420
they can take advantage of aerodynamic forces
to course-correct over most of their flight

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:50.940
path”, making it difficult for enemy radars
to predict where they will land; “ballistic

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:56.290
missiles, in contrast, can execute corrective
maneuvers only for brief periods during their

00:02:56.290 --> 00:03:01.100
initial ascènt out of the atmosphere and
final descent to Earth.”

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:06.180
However, glide vehicles, while having this
bonus, suffer from a lower average speed than

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:12.160
ballistic missiles due to atmospheric flight
and, above all, suffer from extreme overheating

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:17.620
in the subsystems, which not only affects
the aerodynamics of the missile but also blocks

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:23.650
communication with satellites or other guidance
sources, thereby worsening control.

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:28.400
Something that apparently doesn't affect hypersonic
cruise missiles, since they fly slower than

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:30.120
rocket-launched systems.

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:34.970
Beyond the debate surrounding these weapons,
one thing is for sure, they pose a challenge

00:03:34.970 --> 00:03:39.270
to many existing types of medium and long-range
air missile defenses.

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:45.260
U.S. defense officials say this "will require
the development of wholly new intercept systems,

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:48.740
supporting technologies, and a new sensor
architecture".

00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:53.840
The ones of most concern are glide vehicles,
so much so that the U.S. is developing defense

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:59.490
systems focused on eliminating this type of
threat in the end phase of its flight, since,

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:04.610
as expert James Acton claims, it will travel
slower just before reaching its target due

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:09.280
to resistance with the air, thereby being
able to be intercepted more easily.

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:12.850
Who is funding the development of these engineering
marvels?

00:04:12.850 --> 00:04:14.660
Let's take a trip to the United States.

00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:16.250
I'll tell you just a few numbers.

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:22.460
The Trump administration had allocated a total
of nearly $6 billion (5.8) for all hypersonic-related

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:27.180
research and development for fiscal years
2020 and 2021.

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:32.750
In May 2021, the Biden administration, in
its budget request for fiscal year 2022, added

00:04:32.750 --> 00:04:39.910
$3.8 billion with the intention of accelerating
Trump's plans by strengthening conventional

00:04:39.910 --> 00:04:42.060
deterrence against Russia and China.

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:47.320
I say conventional because Washington currently
has no plans to equip hypersonic systems with

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:50.280
nuclear warheads, unlike Moscow and Beijing.

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:54.860
Some Pentagon officials say that the rush
to hypersonic programs is due to the fact

00:04:54.860 --> 00:05:00.200
that current American missile capabilities,
such as the sea-launched Tomahawk cruise missile

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:05.250
or the Joint Air to Surface Standoff missile
(JASSM), lack the maneuverability and speed

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:10.620
necessary to counter evolving adversary threats,
especially those from China.

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:16.010
Others, however, leverage the "competitive"
aspect, meaning that as Michael Griffin reports,

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:20.940
the United States needs to develop such weapons
to "allow it to match what its àdversaries

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:21.940
are doing".

00:05:21.940 --> 00:05:27.010
Then there is a debate in the expert community
about how many weapons the US should deploy.

00:05:27.010 --> 00:05:32.110
Some speak of thousands, "to deter Chinese
hypersonic attacks against carriers and airfields

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:34.950
and other critical targets in the Western
Pacific.”

00:05:34.950 --> 00:05:40.180
Others speak of dozens because they should
be used sparingly and exploited for their

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:41.280
"surgical strike capabilities".

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:46.770
In any case, given the internal differences
of opinion within the Pentagon, hypersonic

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:51.070
weapons will put some pretty significant future
funding challenges on the table.

00:05:51.070 --> 00:05:53.680
Instead, what treasures does China hide?

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:58.960
China has always been reluctant to publicly
reveal information about its nuclear forces,

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:04.160
and likewise, very little is known about Beijing's
development of hypersonic weapons.

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:08.830
Overall, China's motivations appear to be
to prevail over American missile defenses

00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:12.890
and outclass its offensive weapons in the
event of a war in Asia.

00:06:12.890 --> 00:06:17.870
According to expert Tong Zhao, almost all
Chinese experts agree that "the ability to

00:06:17.870 --> 00:06:23.020
penetrate defensive systems is the most important
feature of hypersonic weapons."

00:06:23.020 --> 00:06:28.040
There are at least five programs underway
in China, including the Dongfeng-17 (DF-17),

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:34.711
a medium-range ballistic missile that U.S.
officials estimate has a range of 1800 to

00:06:34.711 --> 00:06:39.800
2500 and a speed between Mach 5 and Mach 10.

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:45.650
We don't know whether conventional or nuclear,
but U.S. intelligence has rated it as dual-capable.

00:06:45.650 --> 00:06:51.290
Then there would be a nuclear-capable hypersonic
glide missile prototype known as the Xing-Kong

00:06:51.290 --> 00:06:56.130
2/Starry Sky-2 that would be successfully
tested in 2018.

00:06:56.130 --> 00:07:00.860
Instead, Russia has publicly unveiled the
development of hypersonic weapons, saying

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:06.500
they are intended to ensure that Moscow can
evade any future U.S. anti-missile defenses.

00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:12.040
Putin himself said in September 2020 that
"the U.S. withdrawal from the Anti-Ballistic

00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:16.870
Missile Treaty in 2002 forced Russia to develop
hypersonic weapons."

00:07:16.870 --> 00:07:23.010
And in March 2018, when he introduced a number
of new nuclear weapon launch systems, he also

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:28.460
talked about some hypersonic weapons such
as the Avangard, a hypersonic glide missile

00:07:28.460 --> 00:07:34.440
that was mounted on a SS-19 intercontinental
missile, successfully tested and deployed

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:35.440
in 2019.

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:41.540
Among those intended for use in a regional
context, I would mention the Tsirkon, a sea-launched

00:07:41.540 --> 00:07:46.010
hypersonic cruise missile, and the Kinzhal,
a hypersonic ballistic missile launched from

00:07:46.010 --> 00:07:52.130
the only modified fighter-bomber capable of
doing so, the MIG-31K and deployed a few days

00:07:52.130 --> 00:07:56.800
ago in the Ukrainian village of Deliatyn,
to hit an underground missile and ammunition

00:07:56.800 --> 00:07:57.800
depot.

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:02.480
I open a parenthesis on this, there would
be something strange in Moscow's narrative.

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:06.860
The images released by the Kremlin are of
a drone and it’s difficult to think that

00:08:06.860 --> 00:08:12.080
it could have operated freely in Ukrainian
airspace so far west of the front line, where

00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:17.110
there’s no Russian air superiority and since
Ukrainian air defenses are still standing,

00:08:17.110 --> 00:08:19.460
they could have detected it and shot it down.

00:08:19.460 --> 00:08:24.419
Some journalists then reported that the inhabitants
of the surrounding areas wouldn’t have heard

00:08:24.419 --> 00:08:25.980
any big explosion.

00:08:25.980 --> 00:08:27.130
Make your own judgements.

00:08:27.130 --> 00:08:29.130
But now we come to the conclusion.

00:08:29.130 --> 00:08:34.669
Given the potential risks to stability posed
by hypersonic weapons, arms control is a valuable

00:08:34.669 --> 00:08:38.470
tool for mitigating them but one that may
remain utopian.

00:08:38.470 --> 00:08:42.610
In recent years, experts have begun to explore
several possible options.

00:08:42.610 --> 00:08:47.730
The first measure to ensure strategic stability
is the strengthening of mutual trust between

00:08:47.730 --> 00:08:52.690
powers, which can be translated both in the
collaboration to avoid dangerous consequences

00:08:52.690 --> 00:08:58.070
even in the absence of formal treaties and
in the increase of transparency, thereby sharing

00:08:58.070 --> 00:09:00.390
technical details and information.

00:09:00.390 --> 00:09:05.529
Another tool is the banning of certain types
of weapons, such as long-range ballistic missiles

00:09:05.529 --> 00:09:11.080
(see the Russian Kinzhal and the American
AGM-183) and where this isn’t possible,

00:09:11.080 --> 00:09:16.339
minimizing the risks posed by these systems
by putting on paper bilateral or trilateral

00:09:16.339 --> 00:09:18.370
agreements limiting their number.

00:09:18.370 --> 00:09:22.360
We do not know what the future holds, but
it's likely that none of these cooperative

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:27.730
arms control concepts will gain support, let
alone become the basis for formal agreements

00:09:27.730 --> 00:09:28.730
between powers.

00:09:28.730 --> 00:09:33.770
Well, we are done for today, thank you for
your attention and see you in the next video.

00:09:33.770 --> 00:09:34.270
Ciao!

